Gradient-based hyperparameter optimization is popular in few-shot meta-learning, but its use in tasks with many gradient steps (long horizons) is limited by memory and gradient degradation. While online hyperparameter learning offers a workaround, it introduces greediness and compromises performance.

We introduce Forward-mode Differentiation with Sharing (FDS), an efficient algorithm that resolves these issues. FDS uses forward-mode differentiation to manage memory and shares contiguous hyperparameters to combat gradient degradation. We theoretically demonstrate FDS's noise reduction capabilities and empirically show its efficiency by differentiating through approximately $10^4$ unrolled optimization steps.

On CIFAR-10, FDS significantly outperforms greedy gradient-based methods across wide hyperparameter search ranges, and achieves a 20x speedup over state-of-the-art black-box approaches. Code is available at: \url{https://github.com/polo5/FDS}
